==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|frm|sociable}}, from {{der|en|la|sociābilis}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsoʊʃəbəl/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-sociable.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|of a person}} Tending to [[socialize]] or be [[social]].
#: {{syn|en|friendly|inviting|congenial}}
#: {{ux|en|He's normally pretty quiet, but he gets much more '''sociable''' around women.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Cymbeline|text=Society is no comfort to one not '''sociable'''.}}
# Offering opportunities for conversation; characterized by much conversation.
#: {{ux|en|a '''sociable''' party}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} Capable of being, or fit to be, united in one body or company; [[associable]].
#* {{RQ:Hooker Laws
|passage=They are '''sociable''' parts united into one body.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} No longer hostile; friendly.
#* {{RQ:Beaumont Fletcher Comedies and Tragedies|Philaster|passage=Is the King '''sociable''' And bids thee live ?}}

====Antonyms====
* {{sense|tending to socialize}} {{l|en|unsociable}}

